1
00:00:08,200 --> 00:00:13,760
But he went up from the harbor by a rough track
through wooded country, along the heights, where Athena

2
00:00:13,760 --> 00:00:17,060
had shown him the noble swineherd — the man who cared

3
00:00:17,060 --> 00:00:21,500
for his substance beyond all the
house-slaves noble Odysseus had acquired.

4
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:23,820
He found him sitting in the fore-court, where his yard

5
00:00:23,820 --> 00:00:27,640
was built up high, in a place
with a clear view all around,

6
00:00:27,640 --> 00:00:31,540
big and handsome, with a cleared
run round it. The swineherd

7
00:00:31,540 --> 00:00:37,640
had built it himself for the swine of his absent master —
without his mistress, without old Laertes —

8
00:00:37,640 --> 00:00:41,260
out of quarried stones, and coped it with wild pear;

9
00:00:41,260 --> 00:00:45,020
and outside he had driven stakes the
whole circuit, this way and that,

10
00:00:45,020 --> 00:00:50,860
close-set and thick, split out of the black heart of
the oak; and inside the yard he had made twelve sties

11
00:00:50,860 --> 00:00:56,560
hard by one another, bedding for the swine;
and in each one fifty wallowing sows were penned,

12
00:00:56,560 --> 00:01:03,120
breeding females. The boars slept outside,
far fewer: for the godlike suitors kept thinning them

13
00:01:03,120 --> 00:01:07,800
at their feasts — the swineherd forever
sending in the best of all the fatted hogs;

14
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:10,140
and these numbered three hundred and sixty.

15
00:01:10,140 --> 00:01:16,600
And beside them four dogs always slept — like wild
beasts, raised by the swineherd, a leader of men.

16
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:21,980
He himself was fitting sandals to his feet,
cutting them from a well-tanned oxhide. The others

17
00:01:21,980 --> 00:01:26,720
had gone their ways, this man here,
that man there, with the herded swine —

18
00:01:26,720 --> 00:01:29,200
three of them; the fourth he had sent off to the city

19
00:01:29,200 --> 00:01:32,680
to bring a hog to the overweening suitors — forced to it —

20
00:01:32,680 --> 00:01:35,760
so they might slaughter it and
glut their hearts with meat.

21
00:01:35,760 --> 00:01:38,460
Suddenly the baying dogs caught sight of Odysseus:

22
00:01:38,460 --> 00:01:45,180
they rushed him, yelping. And Odysseus sat down —
pure craft — and the staff dropped from his hand.

23
00:01:45,180 --> 00:01:48,320
There, at his own farm,
he would have suffered shameful hurt,

24
00:01:48,320 --> 00:01:51,380
but the swineherd came fast on his quick feet after them,

25
00:01:51,380 --> 00:01:54,640
darting through the gateway —
the hide flew from his hand —

26
00:01:54,640 --> 00:02:00,660
and shouting he scattered the dogs this way and that
with a shower of stones. And he addressed his master:

27
00:02:00,660 --> 00:02:03,540
"Old man — the dogs came one breath short of tearing you

28
00:02:03,540 --> 00:02:06,660
to pieces, and the shame of it would have poured on me.

29
00:02:06,660 --> 00:02:10,300
And the gods have given me griefs
enough besides, and groaning:

30
00:02:10,300 --> 00:02:14,180
here I sit, mourning a godlike master,
and fatten hogs for other men

31
00:02:14,180 --> 00:02:20,320
to eat, while he, perhaps, is starving for bread and
wanders the lands and towns of men of alien speech —

32
00:02:20,320 --> 00:02:23,580
if indeed he lives still, and sees the light of the sun.

33
00:02:23,580 --> 00:02:28,560
But follow me — into the hut, old man, so that you too,
when you have had your fill of bread and wine,

34
00:02:28,560 --> 00:02:32,220
may say where you come from,
and all the cares you have borne."

35
00:02:32,220 --> 00:02:35,160
So speaking the noble swineherd led him to the hut,

36
00:02:35,160 --> 00:02:38,520
brought him in and set him down:
he heaped springy brushwood

37
00:02:38,520 --> 00:02:44,660
and spread on it the skin of a shaggy wild goat —
his own bedding, big and thick. And Odysseus was glad

38
00:02:44,660 --> 00:02:48,540
that he received him so, and spoke a word, and named him:

39
00:02:48,540 --> 00:02:51,340
"May Zeus grant you, stranger,
and the other deathless gods,

40
00:02:51,340 --> 00:02:55,360
whatever you wish most — since you
welcomed me with all your heart."

41
00:02:55,360 --> 00:02:58,040
And you answered him, Eumaeus the swineherd:

42
00:02:58,040 --> 00:03:01,260
"Stranger, it is not right for me —
not though a worse man came —

43
00:03:01,260 --> 00:03:05,660
to slight a stranger:
for from Zeus they come, all of them,

44
00:03:05,660 --> 00:03:08,720
strangers and beggars; and a gift, though small, is dear —

45
00:03:08,720 --> 00:03:13,980
from such as we are: for that is the way of slaves,
always afraid, when the masters who hold the power

46
00:03:13,980 --> 00:03:18,280
are the young ones. As for him —
the gods have bound his homecoming,

47
00:03:18,280 --> 00:03:21,780
the man who would have loved me devotedly,
and given me possessions —

48
00:03:21,780 --> 00:03:25,500
a house, a plot of land,
a wife that many men had courted —

49
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:28,080
such things as a kind-hearted master gives his house-slave

50
00:03:28,080 --> 00:03:31,680
who has labored hard for him,
whose work a god makes prosper,

51
00:03:31,680 --> 00:03:35,360
the way this work of mine prospers,
at which I hold my post.

52
00:03:35,360 --> 00:03:38,820
For that my master would have paid
me richly, had he grown old here.

53
00:03:38,820 --> 00:03:43,600
But he is dead. — And I wish
Helen's whole breed had gone down

54
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:48,060
on its knees to destruction,
for she loosed the knees of many men:

55
00:03:48,060 --> 00:03:55,300
he too went off for the sake of Agamemnon's honor
to Ilium of the fine horses, to fight the Trojans."

56
00:03:55,300 --> 00:03:58,340
So speaking he quickly belted in his tunic with his sash

57
00:03:58,340 --> 00:04:02,360
and went to the sties, where the
tribes of young pigs were penned.

58
00:04:02,360 --> 00:04:04,840
He took two out of there, and slaughtered them both,

59
00:04:04,840 --> 00:04:08,300
singed them, cut them small, and threaded them on spits,

60
00:04:08,300 --> 00:04:11,600
and when he had roasted all of it,
he brought and set it by Odysseus

61
00:04:11,600 --> 00:04:15,420
hot on the spits themselves,
and dusted it with white barley,

62
00:04:15,420 --> 00:04:21,560
and mixed honey-sweet wine in an ivy-wood bowl,
and sat down facing him, and pressed him on:

63
00:04:21,560 --> 00:04:24,480
"Eat now, stranger — what house-slaves have to offer:

64
00:04:24,480 --> 00:04:28,260
young pig. The fatted hogs — the suitors eat those,

65
00:04:28,260 --> 00:04:32,200
with no thought in their hearts of
the gods' watching eye, no mercy.

66
00:04:32,200 --> 00:04:35,080
Yet the blessed gods love no ruthless dealing:

67
00:04:35,080 --> 00:04:37,740
they honor justice, and the right acts of men.

68
00:04:37,740 --> 00:04:43,120
Why — even enemies, even raiders, who land on
a foreign coast, and Zeus grants them plunder,

69
00:04:43,120 --> 00:04:48,820
and they fill their ships and set off home — even on
their hearts falls the strong fear of the watching eye.

70
00:04:48,820 --> 00:04:52,740
But these men — these men know something;
they have heard some god's own voice

71
00:04:52,740 --> 00:04:55,320
telling his grim death: that is why they will not woo

72
00:04:55,320 --> 00:04:58,620
in the right way, nor go home
to their own, but at their ease

73
00:04:58,620 --> 00:05:02,180
they devour the goods, high-handed, and spare nothing.

74
00:05:02,180 --> 00:05:08,580
For every night and every day that comes from Zeus,
they slaughter victims — never one, and never only two —

75
00:05:08,580 --> 00:05:11,720
and wine they draw off past all measure, and waste it.

76
00:05:11,720 --> 00:05:17,860
For his livelihood was past speaking. No hero
anywhere had so much — not on the dark mainland,

77
00:05:17,860 --> 00:05:23,840
not on Ithaca itself; no twenty men together have
wealth so great. I will count it through for you:

78
00:05:23,840 --> 00:05:27,440
twelve herds of cattle on the mainland;
as many flocks of sheep;

79
00:05:27,440 --> 00:05:30,600
as many droves of pigs; as many ranging herds of goats

80
00:05:30,600 --> 00:05:33,840
in the care of foreigners and of his own herdsmen there.

81
00:05:33,840 --> 00:05:36,760
And here, at the island's end, wide-ranging herds of goats

82
00:05:36,760 --> 00:05:40,260
graze, eleven in all, with good men watching over them,

83
00:05:40,260 --> 00:05:43,740
and every day each man of them
drives up a beast for those others —

84
00:05:43,740 --> 00:05:48,700
whichever of the fatted goats looks best;
and I keep guard over these sows, and protect them,

85
00:05:48,700 --> 00:05:52,020
and choose out the best of the swine, and send it in."

86
00:05:52,020 --> 00:05:55,800
So he spoke; and Odysseus ate his meat and drank his wine,

87
00:05:55,800 --> 00:05:58,900
ravenous, silent — planting evils for the suitors.

88
00:05:58,900 --> 00:06:04,020
But when he had supped, and steadied his heart with food,
the swineherd filled the cup he drank from himself

89
00:06:04,020 --> 00:06:08,200
brim-full of wine and gave it;
and he took it, glad at heart,

90
00:06:08,200 --> 00:06:11,080
and lifting his voice he spoke winged words to him:

91
00:06:11,080 --> 00:06:16,620
"Friend — who was the man who bought you with his goods,
the master rich and powerful as you describe?

92
00:06:16,620 --> 00:06:19,380
You say he perished for the sake of Agamemnon's honor:

93
00:06:19,380 --> 00:06:23,140
name him — I may know him, being such a one as he was.

94
00:06:23,140 --> 00:06:26,400
For Zeus knows, I suppose, and the other deathless gods,

95
00:06:26,400 --> 00:06:29,580
whether I have seen him. I have wandered far."

96
00:06:29,580 --> 00:06:32,580
Then the swineherd answered him, a leader of men:

97
00:06:32,580 --> 00:06:38,260
"Old man — no wanderer coming with news of him
could persuade his wife now, or his dear son.

98
00:06:38,260 --> 00:06:42,900
Drifters in need of keep — they lie at random,
and have no wish to speak the truth.

99
00:06:42,900 --> 00:06:48,260
Every vagrant who finds his way to the land of
Ithaca goes to my mistress and babbles his deceits;

100
00:06:48,260 --> 00:06:52,620
and she receives him kindly,
and treats him well, and questions everything,

101
00:06:52,620 --> 00:06:55,640
and as she grieves the tears come falling from her lids,

102
00:06:55,640 --> 00:06:59,060
as is the right way of a wife whose husband dies far off.

103
00:06:59,060 --> 00:07:02,800
You too, old man, would have a
story stitched together fast enough

104
00:07:02,800 --> 00:07:06,120
if someone would give you clothing — a cloak and a tunic.

105
00:07:06,120 --> 00:07:08,660
But him — by now the dogs and the quick birds

106
00:07:08,660 --> 00:07:12,180
have torn the skin from his bones,
and the life has gone out of him;

107
00:07:12,180 --> 00:07:15,240
or the fish have eaten him in the sea, and his bones

108
00:07:15,240 --> 00:07:19,500
lie on a shore somewhere, wrapped deep in drifting sand.

109
00:07:19,500 --> 00:07:23,080
So he is dead and gone over there,
and grief is left behind

110
00:07:23,080 --> 00:07:30,780
for all who loved him — for me above all: for never
again will I find a master so gentle, wherever I go —

111
00:07:30,780 --> 00:07:36,980
not though I came again to my father's and my mother's
house, where I was born, and where they reared me.

112
00:07:36,980 --> 00:07:40,460
And it is not even for them I
mourn so much, for all my longing

113
00:07:40,460 --> 00:07:43,460
to see them with my eyes again in my own country:

114
00:07:43,460 --> 00:07:47,800
no — it is longing for Odysseus,
who is gone, that takes hold of me.

115
00:07:47,800 --> 00:07:51,120
I shrink from naming him, stranger, even now he is absent,

116
00:07:51,120 --> 00:07:54,400
for he loved me beyond measure, and cared for me at heart.

117
00:07:54,400 --> 00:07:57,780
I call him 'my beloved lord,' even far away."

118
00:07:57,780 --> 00:08:01,100
Then much-enduring noble Odysseus spoke to him again:

119
00:08:01,100 --> 00:08:07,120
"Friend — since you deny it flat, and say that he will
come no more, and your heart is set in disbelief —

120
00:08:07,120 --> 00:08:10,280
then I will say it not just so, but with an oath upon it:

121
00:08:10,280 --> 00:08:16,560
Odysseus is coming home. And let my reward for the
good news come the moment he arrives at his own halls:

122
00:08:16,560 --> 00:08:19,880
dress me then in a cloak and a tunic, fine clothing.

123
00:08:19,880 --> 00:08:23,940
Before that — though I stand in
sore need — I will take nothing.

124
00:08:23,940 --> 00:08:30,520
For hateful to me as the very gates of Hades is
the man who yields to poverty and babbles deceits.

125
00:08:30,520 --> 00:08:35,020
Now Zeus be my witness, first of the gods,
and this table of friendship,

126
00:08:35,020 --> 00:08:38,340
and the hearth of blameless Odysseus,
to which I have come:

127
00:08:38,340 --> 00:08:41,880
all this shall be accomplished exactly as I say.

128
00:08:41,880 --> 00:08:45,480
Within this very turning of the
light Odysseus will be here:

129
00:08:45,480 --> 00:08:50,840
between this month's waning and the rising of the next,
he will come home; and he will punish whoever

130
00:08:50,840 --> 00:08:54,560
dishonors his wife and his shining son in this place."

131
00:08:54,560 --> 00:08:57,460
And you answered him, Eumaeus the swineherd:

132
00:08:57,460 --> 00:09:00,680
"Old man, I will never be paying
that reward for good news,

133
00:09:00,680 --> 00:09:03,640
and Odysseus will come home no more. Drink your wine

134
00:09:03,640 --> 00:09:06,880
in peace, and let us turn our
minds to other things. Do not

135
00:09:06,880 --> 00:09:12,460
remind me of all this: for the heart in my breast
aches whenever anyone recalls my faithful master.

136
00:09:12,460 --> 00:09:17,960
As for the oath — we will let it be. But may
Odysseus come — as I wish it, as Penelope wishes,

137
00:09:17,960 --> 00:09:21,040
and old Laertes, and godlike Telemachus.

138
00:09:21,040 --> 00:09:24,740
And now it is the boy I mourn
without ceasing — Odysseus' own son

139
00:09:24,740 --> 00:09:30,520
Telemachus. The gods reared him like a young tree,
and I thought he would stand among men no whit behind

140
00:09:30,520 --> 00:09:36,580
his own dear father, a wonder in build and beauty —
and some immortal crippled the balanced mind within him,

141
00:09:36,580 --> 00:09:42,980
or some man did: he has gone for news of his father
to holy Pylos. And the lordly suitors lie in wait

142
00:09:42,980 --> 00:09:49,660
for his homecoming — so that the line of godlike
Arceisius may perish out of Ithaca and leave no name.

143
00:09:49,660 --> 00:09:55,800
Well — we will let him be, whether he is taken or escapes,
and the son of Cronos stretches a hand over him.

144
00:09:55,800 --> 00:10:02,220
But come, old man, tell me your own troubles now;
and tell me this too, honestly, so that I may know it:

145
00:10:02,220 --> 00:10:05,900
What man are you, and from where?
Where are your city and your parents?

146
00:10:05,900 --> 00:10:13,020
On what kind of ship did you come? And how did
sailors bring you to Ithaca? Who did they claim to be?

147
00:10:13,020 --> 00:10:15,800
For I hardly think you came here on foot."

148
00:10:15,800 --> 00:10:18,760
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered him:

149
00:10:18,760 --> 00:10:21,040
"Then I will tell you all of it, and truly.

150
00:10:21,040 --> 00:10:26,620
If only there were food for the two of us,
for a good while, and sweet wine here inside the hut,

151
00:10:26,620 --> 00:10:31,860
to feast on quietly while others did the work —
then I could go on easily for a full year

152
00:10:31,860 --> 00:10:37,940
and still not finish out the tale of my heart's cares,
all that I have labored through by the gods' will.

153
00:10:37,940 --> 00:10:40,960
Out of wide Crete I claim my lineage:

154
00:10:40,960 --> 00:10:46,800
a rich man's son. Many other sons besides
were born and bred in his hall, true-born,

155
00:10:46,800 --> 00:10:50,580
sons of his wife. My mother was bought — a concubine.

156
00:10:50,580 --> 00:10:56,980
Yet Castor, Hylax' son — the man whose blood I claim —
honored me equally with his lawful sons.

157
00:10:56,980 --> 00:11:03,760
In those days the Cretan people honored him like a god
for his fortune, his wealth, and his glorious sons.

158
00:11:03,760 --> 00:11:09,440
But the death-spirits came and carried him away
to the house of Hades. And his high-hearted sons

159
00:11:09,440 --> 00:11:15,720
divided up the living and cast lots for it,
and to me they gave a pittance, and allotted me a house.

160
00:11:15,720 --> 00:11:22,220
Still, I won myself a wife from a family of wide
holdings — by my own worth: for I was no empty man,

161
00:11:22,220 --> 00:11:28,120
no shirker of the fight. Now it is all gone from me;
but even so, I think, looking at the stubble

162
00:11:28,120 --> 00:11:33,080
you can tell the standing grain. Since then,
trouble past measure has held me.

163
00:11:33,080 --> 00:11:38,680
Ares and Athena, yes, they gave me boldness and the
strength that breaks men's ranks. Whenever I picked

164
00:11:38,680 --> 00:11:45,300
the best men for an ambush, sowing evils for the enemy,
my proud heart never once looked forward to death:

165
00:11:45,300 --> 00:11:48,800
far the first, I would leap out
and take my man with the spear —

166
00:11:48,800 --> 00:11:51,420
whichever of the enemy gave me his back in flight.

167
00:11:51,420 --> 00:11:57,460
Such I was in war. But field-work I never loved,
nor the housekeeping that rears shining children:

168
00:11:57,460 --> 00:12:03,920
what I loved, always, was the oared ships,
and wars, and polished javelins, and arrows —

169
00:12:03,920 --> 00:12:06,500
grim things, that other men shudder at.

170
00:12:06,500 --> 00:12:09,980
But I loved what the god, I suppose, set in my heart:

171
00:12:09,980 --> 00:12:13,040
one man delights in one work, another in another.

172
00:12:13,040 --> 00:12:18,620
For before the sons of the Achaeans set foot on Troy,
nine times I led men and fast-running ships

173
00:12:18,620 --> 00:12:21,660
against foreign peoples, and rich takings came my way.

174
00:12:21,660 --> 00:12:25,120
From it I drew out my pleasure, and much fell to me

175
00:12:25,120 --> 00:12:32,180
by lot besides. My house grew great at once,
and thereafter I walked among the Cretans feared and respected.

176
00:12:32,180 --> 00:12:38,060
But when Zeus of the wide voice devised at last
that hateful road that loosed the knees of many men,

177
00:12:38,060 --> 00:12:43,340
then they pressed me and glorious Idomeneus to
lead the ships to Ilium. There was no device

178
00:12:43,340 --> 00:12:46,880
for refusing: the people's hard talk held us to it.

179
00:12:46,880 --> 00:12:52,540
There we sons of the Achaeans made war nine years,
and in the tenth we sacked Priam's city and set off

180
00:12:52,540 --> 00:12:55,940
home with our ships; and a god scattered the Achaeans.

181
00:12:55,940 --> 00:13:00,260
But for me — wretched me —
Zeus the deviser designed evils.

182
00:13:00,260 --> 00:13:05,900
One month, no more, I stayed, delighting in my children,
my wedded wife, and my possessions. And then

183
00:13:05,900 --> 00:13:10,760
my heart bade me fit out ships and sail
for Egypt, with my godlike companions.

184
00:13:10,760 --> 00:13:14,120
Nine ships I fitted out, and the crews gathered fast.

185
00:13:14,120 --> 00:13:18,400
Six days after that my trusted companions
feasted, and I gave victims in plenty

186
00:13:18,400 --> 00:13:22,220
for sacrifice to the gods,
and for the men to make their feast.

187
00:13:22,220 --> 00:13:27,620
On the seventh we boarded, and from wide Crete
we sailed with a fresh fair North Wind behind us,

188
00:13:27,620 --> 00:13:32,540
easily, as if downstream. No ship
of mine took harm: unhurt and whole

189
00:13:32,540 --> 00:13:35,880
we sat, and the wind and the
steersmen held them on course.

190
00:13:35,880 --> 00:13:39,360
On the fifth day we reached the
fair-flowing river of Egypt,

191
00:13:39,360 --> 00:13:42,840
and in the river of Egypt I moored my curved ships.

192
00:13:42,840 --> 00:13:49,000
Then, you may be sure, I ordered my trusted companions
to stay there by the ships and guard the ships,

193
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:51,480
and I sent out scouts to the lookout places.

194
00:13:51,480 --> 00:13:54,580
But they gave way to arrogance —
followed their own force —

195
00:13:54,580 --> 00:13:57,040
and straightway began to pillage the beautiful fields

196
00:13:57,040 --> 00:14:00,820
of the Egyptian men: they carried
off the women and small children,

197
00:14:00,820 --> 00:14:04,320
and killed the men. And quickly the cry reached the city.

198
00:14:04,320 --> 00:14:06,860
Hearing the shout, at the showing of dawn the people

199
00:14:06,860 --> 00:14:10,560
came; and the whole plain filled with infantry and horses

200
00:14:10,560 --> 00:14:13,980
and the lightning of bronze.
And Zeus who delights in thunder

201
00:14:13,980 --> 00:14:17,600
threw an evil panic on my companions, and no man dared

202
00:14:17,600 --> 00:14:20,880
stand and face it: for destruction ringed us everywhere.

203
00:14:20,880 --> 00:14:23,760
There they killed many of us with the sharp bronze,

204
00:14:23,760 --> 00:14:28,100
and others they led up alive, to slave for them by force.

205
00:14:28,100 --> 00:14:34,320
But as for me — Zeus himself put this thought in
my mind (would I had died instead, and met my doom

206
00:14:34,320 --> 00:14:37,880
there, in Egypt! for more grief
stood waiting to receive me):

207
00:14:37,880 --> 00:14:41,020
at once I took the well-made helmet from my head,

208
00:14:41,020 --> 00:14:44,740
and the shield from my shoulders,
and dropped the spear from my hand,

209
00:14:44,740 --> 00:14:46,920
and went straight toward the king's chariot,

210
00:14:46,920 --> 00:14:51,160
and clasped his knees and kissed them.
He spared me, and had mercy,

211
00:14:51,160 --> 00:14:54,840
and sat me in his car, and took me, weeping, to his house.

212
00:14:54,840 --> 00:14:59,120
Truly, man after man came
lunging at me with the ash spear,

213
00:14:59,120 --> 00:15:02,360
raging to kill me — for they
were angered beyond all measure —

214
00:15:02,360 --> 00:15:09,420
but he held them off: he feared the wrath of Zeus of
Strangers, who above all feels fury at evil deeds.

215
00:15:09,420 --> 00:15:15,660
There I stayed seven years, and I gathered much
wealth among the Egyptian men: for all of them gave.

216
00:15:15,660 --> 00:15:18,300
But when the eighth year came wheeling round for me,

217
00:15:18,300 --> 00:15:22,240
then there came a Phoenician,
a man well-schooled in deceits —

218
00:15:22,240 --> 00:15:25,620
a gnawer of men, who had already done the world much harm.

219
00:15:25,620 --> 00:15:31,000
He talked me round, that one, and took me with him to
Phoenicia, where his house and his possessions lay,

220
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:34,040
and there I stayed with him for the full round of a year.

221
00:15:34,040 --> 00:15:37,340
But when at last the months and the
days were coming to fulfillment,

222
00:15:37,340 --> 00:15:43,200
as the year rolled round and the seasons came again,
he put me aboard a seagoing ship for Libya —

223
00:15:43,200 --> 00:15:46,520
a lying scheme: I was to help him run a cargo there,

224
00:15:46,520 --> 00:15:50,100
but there he meant to sell me, and take a boundless price.

225
00:15:50,100 --> 00:15:53,640
I went with him aboard — suspecting it, but forced.

226
00:15:53,640 --> 00:15:56,840
And she ran on with a fresh fair North Wind behind her,

227
00:15:56,840 --> 00:16:01,600
on the mid-sea track above Crete.
And Zeus was devising their destruction:

228
00:16:01,600 --> 00:16:07,080
for when we had left Crete behind,
and no other land showed anywhere — only sky and sea —

229
00:16:07,080 --> 00:16:12,660
then the son of Cronos set a dark-blue cloud over
the hollow ship, and the deep went black beneath it.

230
00:16:12,660 --> 00:16:16,600
And Zeus thundered in the same instant,
and hurled his bolt into the ship,

231
00:16:16,600 --> 00:16:20,640
and she shivered stem to stern,
struck by the bolt of Zeus,

232
00:16:20,640 --> 00:16:24,140
and filled with the reek of sulfur;
and all the men fell from the ship.

233
00:16:24,140 --> 00:16:26,860
Like sea-crows around the black hull

234
00:16:26,860 --> 00:16:30,940
they were carried off on the waves;
and the god took away their homecoming.

235
00:16:30,940 --> 00:16:34,620
But as for me — Zeus himself,
though my heart was full of anguish,

236
00:16:34,620 --> 00:16:40,840
put the huge mast of the dark-blue-prowed ship into
my two hands, so that I might yet escape the evil.

237
00:16:40,840 --> 00:16:43,980
Wrapped around it I was carried before the deadly winds.

238
00:16:43,980 --> 00:16:47,880
Nine days I was carried;
and on the tenth, in the black night,

239
00:16:47,880 --> 00:16:50,820
a great rolling wave brought
me near the Thesprotians' land.

240
00:16:50,820 --> 00:16:57,900
There the king of the Thesprotians, the hero Pheidon,
took me in — and asked no price: his own dear son

241
00:16:57,900 --> 00:17:01,920
had found me beaten down by cold
and weariness, and led me home,

242
00:17:01,920 --> 00:17:05,640
raising me up by the hand,
until we reached his father's halls;

243
00:17:05,640 --> 00:17:08,540
and he put clothing on me, a cloak and a tunic.

244
00:17:08,540 --> 00:17:11,540
It was there I heard of Odysseus. For the king said

245
00:17:11,540 --> 00:17:16,420
he had hosted him, a guest and friend,
on his way home to his fathers' land,

246
00:17:16,420 --> 00:17:22,920
and he showed me all the treasure Odysseus had gathered —
bronze and gold and iron worked with labor —

247
00:17:22,920 --> 00:17:28,620
enough to feed a man's line to the tenth generation,
such a store of it lay in the lord's halls.

248
00:17:28,620 --> 00:17:35,140
Odysseus, he said, was gone to Dodona, to hear the
will of Zeus out of the god's own high-crowned oak:

249
00:17:35,140 --> 00:17:41,080
how he should come home to the rich land of Ithaca,
openly or in secret, after so long away.

250
00:17:41,080 --> 00:17:44,320
And he swore an oath to me,
pouring the libation in his house,

251
00:17:44,320 --> 00:17:50,300
that a ship was hauled down and a crew standing
ready to convey him home to his own dear fatherland.

252
00:17:50,300 --> 00:17:56,920
But me he sent off first: for a Thesprotian ship
happened to be sailing for Dulichium, rich in wheat.

253
00:17:56,920 --> 00:18:01,200
He charged them to bring me there,
with all care, to King Acastus.

254
00:18:01,200 --> 00:18:04,980
But an evil counsel about me
pleased their hearts instead —

255
00:18:04,980 --> 00:18:08,940
so that I might yet come down to the last dregs of misery.

256
00:18:08,940 --> 00:18:11,820
When the seagoing ship was far out from the land,

257
00:18:11,820 --> 00:18:15,880
they set about contriving,
then and there, my day of slavery.

258
00:18:15,880 --> 00:18:22,420
They stripped me of my clothing, cloak and tunic both,
and threw around me different gear — this foul rag

259
00:18:22,420 --> 00:18:26,980
and this torn tunic — the very
ones you see with your own eyes;

260
00:18:26,980 --> 00:18:31,380
and at evening they reached the
tilled fields of clear-skied Ithaca.

261
00:18:31,380 --> 00:18:36,260
There they lashed me tight in the well-benched ship
with a twisted rope, and went ashore themselves

262
00:18:36,260 --> 00:18:39,460
and hurried their supper along the margin of the sea.

263
00:18:39,460 --> 00:18:44,760
But the gods themselves undid my lashing —
easily. And wrapping my rag about my head,

264
00:18:44,760 --> 00:18:50,960
I slid down the polished lading-plank, breasted the sea,
then struck out with both arms, swimming,

265
00:18:50,960 --> 00:18:54,340
and very soon I was out of the water, clear of them.

266
00:18:54,340 --> 00:19:00,720
I climbed up where a thicket of flowering brush stood,
and lay there, crouched. And they, groaning loud,

267
00:19:00,720 --> 00:19:05,800
cast about; but there seemed no profit for them
in searching further, so they went back again

268
00:19:05,800 --> 00:19:11,240
aboard their hollow ship. And the gods hid me easily,
and brought me on my way to the steading

269
00:19:11,240 --> 00:19:15,960
of an understanding man.
For it is still my portion to live."

270
00:19:15,960 --> 00:19:18,720
And you answered him, Eumaeus the swineherd:

271
00:19:18,720 --> 00:19:22,160
"Poor luckless stranger —
truly you have stirred the heart in me,

272
00:19:22,160 --> 00:19:26,120
telling all this through, all you
have suffered, all your wandering.

273
00:19:26,120 --> 00:19:29,740
But one part is not in order,
and you will never persuade me —

274
00:19:29,740 --> 00:19:35,460
that about Odysseus. Why should a man like you
lie into the empty air? I know it well myself,

275
00:19:35,460 --> 00:19:38,260
my master's homecoming: how the gods all hated him,

276
00:19:38,260 --> 00:19:41,140
utterly — since they did not
bring him down among the Trojans,

277
00:19:41,140 --> 00:19:45,340
or in the arms of his friends,
once he had wound the war to its end.

278
00:19:45,340 --> 00:19:48,100
Then the whole Achaean people would have built him a tomb,

279
00:19:48,100 --> 00:19:52,060
and he would have won great fame in
time to come for his son as well.

280
00:19:52,060 --> 00:19:55,480
Instead the whirlwinds have
snatched him away without glory.

281
00:19:55,480 --> 00:20:01,980
And I — I keep apart, with my pigs. I do not go to
the city, unless perhaps watchful-minded Penelope

282
00:20:01,980 --> 00:20:05,380
bids me come, when news arrives from somewhere;

283
00:20:05,380 --> 00:20:11,020
then they all sit around the newcomer and question
everything — those who grieve for their long-gone lord,

284
00:20:11,020 --> 00:20:14,780
and those who rejoice, eating up his living unpunished.

285
00:20:14,780 --> 00:20:20,020
But I have no love of asking and inquiring,
ever since an Aetolian cheated me with his tale —

286
00:20:20,020 --> 00:20:23,400
a man who had killed someone,
and wandered over the wide earth,

287
00:20:23,400 --> 00:20:26,900
and came to my house; and I received him with open arms.

288
00:20:26,900 --> 00:20:30,960
He said he had seen him among
the Cretans, at Idomeneus' side,

289
00:20:30,960 --> 00:20:36,380
mending the ships the storms had shattered on him,
and said he would come by summer, or by harvest-time,

290
00:20:36,380 --> 00:20:39,780
bringing much wealth,
together with his godlike companions.

291
00:20:39,780 --> 00:20:43,740
So you, old man of many sorrows —
since some power has brought you to me —

292
00:20:43,740 --> 00:20:46,860
do not court my favor with lies, do not charm me:

293
00:20:46,860 --> 00:20:49,920
it is not for that I will respect you or befriend you,

294
00:20:49,920 --> 00:20:52,100
And out of pity for yourself."

295
00:20:52,100 --> 00:20:55,300
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered him:

296
00:20:55,300 --> 00:20:58,120
"Truly the heart in your breast is past all persuading —

297
00:20:58,120 --> 00:21:03,320
such a man you are: not even my oath
won you over; nothing convinces you.

298
00:21:03,320 --> 00:21:06,460
Come then, let us make a bargain
of it — and above the two of us

299
00:21:06,460 --> 00:21:10,200
let the gods who hold Olympus stand witness on both sides.

300
00:21:10,200 --> 00:21:15,520
If your master comes home, to this very house,
you dress me in a cloak and a tunic, and send me on

301
00:21:15,520 --> 00:21:21,000
to Dulichium, where my own heart longs to be;
and if your master does not come as I say he will,

302
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:24,300
set your slaves on me: pitch me down from the great rock —

303
00:21:24,300 --> 00:21:28,020
so that the next beggar too may
think twice before he cheats."

304
00:21:28,020 --> 00:21:30,740
And the noble swineherd answered him, and said:

305
00:21:30,740 --> 00:21:37,160
"Stranger — and a fine name I would win among
mankind for goodness, now and ever after,

306
00:21:37,160 --> 00:21:44,260
I who led you to my hut and gave you a guest's due,
if then I killed you and took the dear life out of you:

307
00:21:44,260 --> 00:21:49,560
with a clean heart, after that,
I could pray to Zeus the son of Cronos!

308
00:21:49,560 --> 00:21:55,980
But it is supper time. Soon now my companions should
be in, so we can make a savory supper in the hut."

309
00:21:55,980 --> 00:22:01,900
So the two of them spoke such things to one another;
and the swine came near, and the men who herd them.

310
00:22:01,900 --> 00:22:04,800
They shut the sows in their pens for the night's sleep,

311
00:22:04,800 --> 00:22:08,180
and a clamor past speech went up as the swine were folded.

312
00:22:08,180 --> 00:22:11,100
Then the noble swineherd called to his companions:

313
00:22:11,100 --> 00:22:14,260
"Bring the best of the hogs,
so I may slaughter it for a guest

314
00:22:14,260 --> 00:22:17,820
from far away. And we will have some good of it ourselves,

315
00:22:17,820 --> 00:22:21,240
who bear the misery of these
white-tusked swine and suffer for it

316
00:22:21,240 --> 00:22:25,020
so long, while others eat our labor and pay nothing."

317
00:22:25,020 --> 00:22:28,860
So speaking, he split the
firewood with the pitiless bronze,

318
00:22:28,860 --> 00:22:35,300
and they brought in a hog, well fattened, five years old,
and stood it by the hearth. Nor did the swineherd

319
00:22:35,300 --> 00:22:38,840
forget the deathless ones: for his mind was set on good.

320
00:22:38,840 --> 00:22:42,040
No — beginning the rite,
he cast into the fire the head-bristles

321
00:22:42,040 --> 00:22:48,200
of the white-tusked hog, and prayed to all the gods
that wise Odysseus might come home to his own house.

322
00:22:48,200 --> 00:22:51,500
Then he lifted an oak billet,
left over from the splitting,

323
00:22:51,500 --> 00:22:55,420
and struck: and the life left the hog;
and the others cut the throat,

324
00:22:55,420 --> 00:22:58,600
and singed it, and quickly
quartered it out. And the swineherd,

325
00:22:58,600 --> 00:23:04,740
beginning the offering, laid raw flesh from every
limb in the rich fat, and cast the pieces in the fire,

326
00:23:04,740 --> 00:23:08,080
sprinkled over with barley meal; and they carved the rest

327
00:23:08,080 --> 00:23:11,020
and threaded it on spits, and roasted it with care,

328
00:23:11,020 --> 00:23:14,940
and drew it all off, and heaped it
on the platters. And the swineherd

329
00:23:14,940 --> 00:23:18,200
stood up to divide it: for his mind knew what was fair.

330
00:23:18,200 --> 00:23:20,540
Into seven portions he divided the whole:

331
00:23:20,540 --> 00:23:23,960
one he set aside, with a prayer,
for the Nymphs and for Hermes,

332
00:23:23,960 --> 00:23:29,880
Maia's son; the rest he served out to each man;
and Odysseus he honored with the long unbroken chine

333
00:23:29,880 --> 00:23:33,640
of the white-tusked hog —
and made his master's heart proud.

334
00:23:33,640 --> 00:23:36,600
And Odysseus of the many designs
lifted his voice and said:

335
00:23:36,600 --> 00:23:39,520
"Eumaeus — may you be as dear to father Zeus

336
00:23:39,520 --> 00:23:43,860
as you are to me: for you honor such
a man as I am now with the best."

337
00:23:43,860 --> 00:23:46,520
And you answered him, Eumaeus the swineherd:

338
00:23:46,520 --> 00:23:49,640
"Eat, my god-touched guest,
and take your pleasure in such things

339
00:23:49,640 --> 00:23:53,320
as are here. The god will give
one thing and let another go,

340
00:23:53,320 --> 00:23:56,780
whatever his own heart wills: for he can do all things."

341
00:23:56,780 --> 00:24:00,440
He spoke, and burned the first-cuts
to the gods who are forever,

342
00:24:00,440 --> 00:24:03,280
and poured the fire-bright wine,
and set the cup in the hands

343
00:24:03,280 --> 00:24:07,580
of Odysseus, sacker of cities;
and sat down to his own portion.

344
00:24:07,580 --> 00:24:14,100
And Mesaulius served them bread — a man the swineherd
had got for himself, alone, while his master was away,

345
00:24:14,100 --> 00:24:16,080
without his mistress, without old Laertes:

346
00:24:16,080 --> 00:24:20,260
he had bought him from the Taphians with goods of his own.

347
00:24:20,260 --> 00:24:23,480
And their hands went out to the
good things spread before them.

348
00:24:23,480 --> 00:24:28,860
But when they had put away desire for food and drink,
Mesaulius took away the bread; and they, filled

349
00:24:28,860 --> 00:24:32,440
full of bread and meat, bestirred themselves for bed.

350
00:24:32,440 --> 00:24:36,520
And the night came on, foul,
at the moon's dark, and Zeus rained

351
00:24:36,520 --> 00:24:41,200
the whole night through, and the wet
West Wind blew big without ceasing.

352
00:24:41,200 --> 00:24:44,800
Then Odysseus spoke among them, testing the swineherd —

353
00:24:44,800 --> 00:24:47,720
whether he would strip off his own cloak and give it,

354
00:24:47,720 --> 00:24:51,980
or urge it out of one of his companions,
since he cared for him so:

355
00:24:51,980 --> 00:24:55,300
"Hear me now, Eumaeus, and all you other companions,

356
00:24:55,300 --> 00:24:58,740
while I speak a word of boasting: it is the crazing wine

357
00:24:58,740 --> 00:25:02,360
that commands me — wine,
that sets even the deep-minded singing

358
00:25:02,360 --> 00:25:09,000
and giggling softly, and stirs him up to dance,
and tosses out many a word better left unspoken.

359
00:25:09,000 --> 00:25:13,120
Still, since I have blurted once, I will hide nothing.

360
00:25:13,120 --> 00:25:15,740
Would I were young, and my strength stood firm in me,

361
00:25:15,740 --> 00:25:19,380
as on the day we laid our ambush and led it under Troy.

362
00:25:19,380 --> 00:25:25,540
Odysseus led, and Atreus' son Menelaus, and with them
I was third in command: they asked it themselves.

363
00:25:25,540 --> 00:25:30,500
When we had come up to the city and the steep wall,
we lay around the town in the dense brushwood,

364
00:25:30,500 --> 00:25:33,400
crouched under our arms among the reeds and marsh-grass,

365
00:25:33,400 --> 00:25:37,100
and a foul night came on, the North Wind dropping down,

366
00:25:37,100 --> 00:25:43,800
freezing; and snow came falling on us like hoarfrost,
bitter cold, and ice crusted thick on the shields.

367
00:25:43,800 --> 00:25:46,820
Then all the others had their cloaks and tunics,

368
00:25:46,820 --> 00:25:49,660
and slept at their ease,
shoulders wrapped in their shields;

369
00:25:49,660 --> 00:25:55,600
but I had left my cloak behind with my companions —
fool that I was — never thinking I would freeze:

370
00:25:55,600 --> 00:25:59,120
I had come with only my shield and my shining waist-guard.

371
00:25:59,120 --> 00:26:02,100
But in the night's third watch,
when the stars had crossed their height,

372
00:26:02,100 --> 00:26:05,460
then I spoke to Odysseus — he was there beside me —

373
00:26:05,460 --> 00:26:08,760
nudging him with my elbow; and he listened on the instant:

374
00:26:08,760 --> 00:26:14,820
'Zeus-born son of Laertes, Odysseus of the many devices,
I shall not be among the living long: this cold

375
00:26:14,820 --> 00:26:21,300
is killing me. I have no cloak. Some power tricked
me into a single tunic. And now there is no way out.'

376
00:26:21,300 --> 00:26:27,480
So I spoke; and he then held this plan in his heart —
such a man he was for counsel, and for fighting —

377
00:26:27,480 --> 00:26:30,140
and speaking low, close in my ear, he said:

378
00:26:30,140 --> 00:26:32,580
'Quiet now, lest some other Achaean hear you.'

379
00:26:32,580 --> 00:26:35,300
Then he propped his head on his elbow and spoke out:

380
00:26:35,300 --> 00:26:38,640
'Hear me, friends. A divine dream came to me in my sleep.

381
00:26:38,640 --> 00:26:41,520
We have come out too far from the ships. Let someone go

382
00:26:41,520 --> 00:26:47,560
and tell Atreus' son Agamemnon, shepherd of the people,
to send more men from the ships to join us here.'

383
00:26:47,560 --> 00:26:53,080
So he spoke; and up jumped Thoas, Andraemon's son,
fast, and flung off his purple cloak,

384
00:26:53,080 --> 00:26:56,440
and set off running for the ships.
And I, in that man's garment,

385
00:26:56,440 --> 00:26:59,860
lay glad until Dawn shone on her golden throne.

386
00:26:59,860 --> 00:27:02,900
Would I were young like that now,
and my strength stood firm:

387
00:27:02,900 --> 00:27:05,860
then someone in this steading
might give a swineherd's cloak —

388
00:27:05,860 --> 00:27:09,940
both ways at once: in kindness,
and in respect for a good man.

389
00:27:09,940 --> 00:27:13,220
As it is, they scorn me for the foul clothes on my skin."

390
00:27:13,220 --> 00:27:15,420
And you answered him, Eumaeus the swineherd:

391
00:27:15,420 --> 00:27:17,980
"Old man — that is a blameless fable you have told:

392
00:27:17,980 --> 00:27:21,400
not one word of it off the measure;
nothing said without its profit.

393
00:27:21,400 --> 00:27:28,220
So you shall not lack for clothing, nor for anything
else that is owed a hard-worn suppliant when he comes —

394
00:27:28,220 --> 00:27:32,300
tonight. But at dawn you will knock
about in those rags of yours again:

395
00:27:32,300 --> 00:27:37,940
for there are not many cloaks here, or changes
of tunic, to put on — one only for each man.

396
00:27:37,940 --> 00:27:43,540
But when the dear son of Odysseus comes,
he will give you clothing himself, a cloak and a tunic,

397
00:27:43,540 --> 00:27:46,520
and send you wherever your heart and spirit bid you go."

398
00:27:46,520 --> 00:27:52,860
So speaking he sprang up and set a bed for him close
by the fire, and threw on it skins of sheep and goats.

399
00:27:52,860 --> 00:27:56,880
There Odysseus lay down; and over
him the swineherd threw a cloak,

400
00:27:56,880 --> 00:28:03,120
thick-woven and big, that he kept by him for a change
of clothing, to put on when some terrible storm arose.

401
00:28:03,120 --> 00:28:09,160
So Odysseus slept there, and beside him slept the
young men. But the swineherd — a bed in that place

402
00:28:09,160 --> 00:28:16,240
did not please him, to sleep apart from his swine;
he armed himself to go outside. And Odysseus was glad

403
00:28:16,240 --> 00:28:20,660
that he took such care of his
substance while he was far away.

404
00:28:20,660 --> 00:28:24,340
First he slung his sharp sword over his sturdy shoulders,

405
00:28:24,340 --> 00:28:27,980
then wrapped himself in a
wind-breaking cloak, thick-woven,

406
00:28:27,980 --> 00:28:34,080
and took up the fleece of a big well-fattened goat,
and took his sharp javelin, to hold off dogs and men,

407
00:28:34,080 --> 00:28:40,248
and went out to lie where the white-tusked boars slept,
under a hollow rock, in the lee of the North Wind.
